==== OEM easy installation ====

// Note: Instructions are based on Netgear Firmware 1.0.4.2. They should work for newer versions as well.//

  - Disconnect the device from the Internet.
  - Factory Reset the device.
  - Connect to the device. Either via Ethernet cable to a LAN port or the default WiFI (//NETGEAR-1//). The default WiFI passphrase is //sharedsecret//.  (Do NOT connect any cable into the WAN port)
  - Browse to http://192.168.0.100. You should be forced into the setup wizard.
  - Set a password for the root account. 
  - Skip the Setup Wizard. Confirm you really want to skip the setup wizard.
  - Once at the main administration page, on the left menu, choose ADVANCED > Administration > Firmware Update. The Firmware Update page displays.
  - In the manual update, Click Choose File. Choose the 'Firmware OpenWrt Install'  squashfs-**factory**.img file from your computer. Click OK. 
  - The flashing to openwrt image completes in 3-5 minutes. The device should automatically reboot when flashing is complete. If not, wait 5 minutes and manually power off/power on the device.
  - Browse to http://192.168.1.1

==== OEM installation using nmrpflash ====

The nmrpflash utility can be used to flash a new image via the bootloader.  Both OEM firmware images and OpenWrt factory images can be installed in this fashion.  For experienced users, this method will likely be easier and faster than the web-based method above.

You must connect to the LAN1 port to use nmrpflash.  Note that the bootloader initializes the LAN1 port in 10/100 mode, which means you may need a crossover Ethernet cable if you are connecting the device directly to your computer.

===== Upgrading OpenWrt =====

==== LuCI Web Upgrade Process ====
  - Login to your router's admin page
  - Choose System > Backup/Flash Firmware from the menu at the top.
  - Install the 'Firmware OpenWrt Upgrade' squashfs-sysupgrade.bin file.

==== Terminal Upgrade Process ====

If you don't have a GUI (LuCI) available, you can alternatively upgrade via the command line.
There are two command line methods for upgrading:

  * ''sysupgrade''
  * ''mtd''

Note: It is important that you put the firmware image into the ramdisk (/tmp) before you start flashing.

=== sysupgrade ===

  * Login as root via SSH on your device IP, then enter the following commands:

<code>
cd /tmp
wget http://downloads.openwrt.org/snapshots/trunk/XXX/xxx.abc
sysupgrade /tmp/xxx.abc
</code>

=== mtd ===

If ''sysupgrade'' does not support this router, use ''mtd''.

  * Login as root via SSH on 192.168.1.1, then enter the following commands:

<code>
cd /tmp
wget http://downloads.openwrt.org/snapshots/trunk/XXX/xxx.abc
mtd write /tmp/xxx.abc linux && reboot
</code>

